<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ibbi-Sin</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">r. c. 2028–2004 BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/21st-century-bce">21st century BCE</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Ibbi-Sin was the last king of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/third-dynasty-of-ur">Third Dynasty of Ur</a>, reigning about 2028–2004 BCE. His reign saw the empire break apart as provinces broke away, Amorite incursions and famine struck, and his official <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ishbi-erra">Ishbi-Erra</a> made himself independent at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/isin">Isin</a>. In about 2004 BCE the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/elamites">Elamites</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shimashki-dynasty">Shimashki</a> kings sacked <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ur">Ur</a> and carried Ibbi-Sin captive to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anshan">Anshan</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ibbi-sin.jpg" alt="Ibbi-Sin" width="320" height="367" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Metropolitan Museum of Art · CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ibbi-Sin_enthroned.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>Ibbi-Sin was king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sumer">Sumer</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/akkad">Akkad</a> and the last king of the Ur III dynasty, reigning c. 2028–2004 BCE by the Middle chronology. During his reign <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amorites">Amorites</a> attacked the empire repeatedly. His father <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shu-sin">Shu-Sin</a> had built a wall, the wall of Martu, across <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mesopotamia">Mesopotamia</a> against Amorite incursions, but they penetrated it early in the reign. His 17th year was nevertheless officially named for the Amorites submitting to him. As faith in his leadership failed, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/elam">Elam</a> declared its independence and began to raid as well. He ordered fortifications built at Ur and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nippur">Nippur</a>, but they did not stop the raids or keep the empire unified.</p><p>Scholars have suggested that the kingdom was already in decline through long-term drought, the same drought that may have helped bring down <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/akkad">Akkad</a> c. 2193 BCE, and studies of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persian-gulf">Persian Gulf</a> sediments indicate that the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tigris">Tigris</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/euphrates">Euphrates</a> ran very low around 2100–2000 BCE. In years seven and eight of his reign the price of grain rose to 60 times the norm, which suggests that the success of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amorites">Amorites</a> owed something to attacks on the agricultural and irrigation systems. Cities fell away from a king who could not protect them, notably <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/isin">Isin</a> under the Amorite ruler <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ishbi-erra">Ishbi-Erra</a>. By the end Ibbi-Sin held only the city of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ur">Ur</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>The attacks brought famine and economic collapse, which allowed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/elam">Elam</a> under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kindattu">Kindattu</a> to strike at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ur">Ur</a> and capture the king. Elam, with tribesmen from the region of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shimashki-dynasty">Shimashki</a> in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zagros-mountains">Zagros Mountains</a>, sacked Ur and took Ibbi-Sin captive to Elam, where he was imprisoned and died at an unknown date. The Lament for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sumer">Sumer</a> and Ur tells that the gods An, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/enlil">Enlil</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/enki">Enki</a> and Ninhursaja decided its fate and that Ibbi-Sin was taken in fetters to Elam, never to return to his city. All of his year names are known and document the major events of his reign, among them the destruction of Simurrum and the building of the great walls of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nippur">Nippur</a> and Ur.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Ibbi-Sin” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Its first major offensive success was the sack of Ur around 2004 BCE, when the Shimashki king Kindattu captured <b>Ibbi-Sin</b>, the last ruler of the Third Dynasty of Ur, and ended that dynasty's domination of Elam.</blockquote><a href="/empire/elamite-confederation">Elamite Confederation · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>After <b>Ibbi-Sin</b>'s fifth year his year names stop at Lagash, a sign that Ur had lost the city.</blockquote><a href="/empire/lagash">Lagash · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>The centralised system was efficient but vulnerable to disruption, particularly as provincial governors asserted their autonomy during the reign of the last king, <b>Ibbi-Sin</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/neo-sumerian-empire">Neo-Sumerian Empire · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>The Third Dynasty's own end came by arms, when the Elamites took the city and carried off King <b>Ibbi-Sin</b>, the event mourned in the Lament for Ur.</blockquote><a href="/empire/ur">Ur · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>…from the temple library at Nippur in the University of Pennsylvania Museum; George Barton translated them in 1918 as &quot;A prayer for the city of Ur&quot;, noting that it was plausible but unproven that the prayer was written in the last days of <b>Ibbi-Sin</b>, as Ur was falling.</blockquote><a href="/songs/lament-for-ur">Lament for Ur</a></li><li><blockquote>Ishbi-Erra, an official from Mari serving <b>Ibbi-Sin</b>, last king of the Neo-Sumerian Empire, made himself independent at Isin around 2017 BCE as Ur's grip failed.</blockquote><a href="/state/isin">Isin</a></li></ul></section>
